                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "I enjoyed \"Legends of Eisenwald\" back in the day. Even in 2015, its gameplay mechanics felt like something from the early 2000s. It was clunky, visually rough, and not particularly user-friendly. A solid mid-tier game, but one where you could tell the developers had put their heart into it. Despite its flaws, it kept me interested until the very end, and I also finished the \"Road to Iron Forest\" expansion.\r\n\r\nSo what exactly is \"Eisenwald: Blood of November\"? To me, it feels like another custom map for the original game, or at best a small mod that somehow ended up being released as a standalone title.\r\n\r\nThere's a fairly large map with plenty of points of interest, two major opposing factions, and several minor ones. You can explore freely, complete quests, listen to rumors in taverns, and use them to hunt for hidden treasures. The main story is a satire of the 2016 US presidential election, with Donald Trump and Hillary Clinton barely disguised as the faction leaders. The writers poke fun at both politicians and voters, while a mysterious \"Tsar of Moscow\" lurks in the background, eventually offering his own deal under certain circumstances.\r\n\r\nOnce you join a faction, your main objective is to capture and hold all enemy castles until November 8 on the in-game calendar. There's also a third option: accept the Moscow Tsar's offer, declare war on everyone, and conquer every castle on the map, thankfully without a time limit. Each faction leader has three unique quests with some branching choices, but the supposed nonlinearity mostly comes down to minor decisions within individual quests, without any significant consequences.\r\n\r\nI played this game in 2026, and by then, its political satire felt much more bitter than funny. Still, I have no particular complaints about the story.\r\n\r\nThe gameplay, however, is a different matter. The biggest problem is the endless spawning of neutral parties and Raubritters, all of whom grow progressively stronger as time passes. Regular neutral parties are at least confined to certain areas, but Raubritters can roam across the entire map, capturing everything in their path. I also got the impression that their strength scales with the player's army. As a result, regular castle garrisons, which you've spent your hard-earned money on, are practically useless. Without your main, fully equipped and leveled-up troops, they stand little chance.\r\n\r\nInstead of an engaging campaign, you end up in an endless Benny Hill chase: capture one castle, rush off to retake another, return to defend the first, sell your loot along the way, heal at a monastery, and repeat. It's tolerable the first ten times, but eventually, all this running around and the sheer number of repetitive battles become exhausting. The entire gameplay loop could be summed up as: \"If you want something done right, do it yourself.\"\r\n\r\nThere are hardly any new mechanics. There's a rudimentary building system that lets you unlock additional facilities at your family castle, including a few extra quest chains. You can also invest a considerable amount of money into automated patrols. The problem is that early on, you can't really justify the expense, and by the late game, these patrols are so weak that even ordinary neutral parties can wipe them out. Yet another mechanic that turns out to be practically useless.\r\n\r\nThe technical problems from the original game haven't gone anywhere, either. The old graphical glitches, freezes, and crashes are still here, along with new oddities like multiple enemy units occupying the same hex.\r\n\r\nI still don't understand why this needed to be a standalone release. In terms of content, it's comparable to \"Road to Iron Forest\", except with a much more tedious gameplay loop. Endless enemy spawns, poor balancing, and mechanics that become effectively pointless make the campaign feel artificially stretched out. Even if you go in expecting nothing more than a small DLC, \"Blood of November\" is simply too boring and frustrating for me to recommend buying it.",
